WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.970
- Welcome to the 32nd
Kuznets Memorial Lecture,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.980
organized by Economic Growth Center.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.060
I'm Rohini Pande,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.220
the Director of the
Economic Growth Center,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.560
and a professor in Economics Department.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.050
I'd also like to thank those
who are joining us online.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.030
I gather we have a record number of people

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.860
who've chosen to do so.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.680
Today's talk is being
broadcast and also recorded.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.590
We'll invite everyone's
participation in the Q&amp;A,

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.020
but remind you that by
being present here today,

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.120
you consent to have your comments recorded

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.293
and posted publicly.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.340
So the Kuznets Memorial Lecture
is an annual talk focusing

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.290
on issues in economic development,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.230
which the Growth Center hosts
in honor of Simon Kuznets,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.030
the Nobel Prize winning economist

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.343
who helped found the center in 1961.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.790
Among Kuznets, many
achievements was coming up

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.500
with the original formulation

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.020
of gross domestic product or GDP,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.503
which he presented in a report
to the US Congress in 1937.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.060
GDP was soon widely used to
measure economic development,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.720
but Kuznets himself called
his own measure into question.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.900
As early as 1934, he cautioned
against a narrow focus

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.257
on GDP saying,

00:01:11.257 --> 00:01:13.620
"The welfare of a nation
can scarcely be inferred

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.110
from a measurement of national income."

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.037
In 1962, he added,

00:01:18.037 --> 00:01:20.580
"Distinctions must be kept
in mind between quantity

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.760
and quality of growth between
its costs and returns,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.370
and between the short and long term."

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.200
Today, we're going to hear from Eliana

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.440
about ideas that illuminate
this complicated relationship,

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.590
between the quantity
and quality of growth.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.960
Eliana's groundbreaking research has shown

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.630
that customs beliefs
and other social norms,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.150
can disrupt the link between
wealth and wellbeing,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.340
and increase in equality between groups,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.380
even as countries get richer.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.550
Her work highlights the fact

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.190
that we can't understand
economic development

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.420
without recognizing the role

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.910
of informal norm-based institutions,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.770
family groups, immigrant communities,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.620
traditional gender roles, and others.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.690
Eliana is the professor of public policy

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.280
at Harvard Kennedy School.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.960
She will also be the incoming president

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.300
of the Econometric Society in 2024,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.480
a society that Yale has
many connections with.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.450
In 2020, Eliana received
the Birgit Grodal Award,

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.620
which is awarded annually

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.720
by the European Economic Association,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.000
to a woman who has made
significant contributions

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.400
to the economics profession.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.040
Personally, I've always been a
huge admirer of Eliana's work

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.540
and how thoughtfully she has worked

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.190
to improve our profession.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.920
I'm delighted that she has
agreed to spend this week with us

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.470
to anchor the Kuznets
Mini-Conference tomorrow,

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.740
which has brought many young
leading scholars to our campus,

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.960
and to deliver the 32nd Kuznets Lecture

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.700
on changing harmful norms.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.763
Please join me in welcoming
Eliana La Ferrara.

00:02:40.763 --> 00:02:43.930
(audience applauding)

00:02:47.626 --> 00:02:48.459
- Thank you, Rohini.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:52.710
And so this is an incredible honor for me,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:56.070
so I'm grateful to you, to the EGC,

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.190
to the department for the
hospitality these days.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:00.480
Also, the opportunity

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.776
to organize this mini-conference tomorrow,

00:03:03.776 --> 00:03:06.360
was so welcome.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.690
And you know, it allowed me to connect

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.060
with many of the young researchers

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.890
that I've interacted with
in my first life in Europe.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.230
And so, you will see for those of you

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.760
who are around tomorrow,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.070
that there's a lot of commonality

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.810
and a lot of bridges that I hope

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:29.810
we can continue creating between
development economists here

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.930
and in the European continent.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.930
So, I'd like to start
motivating a bit the choice

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.660
of the topic for this lecture,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.810
and why I think it's
important to talk about norms

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.880
if we care about development.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.130
And as economists,

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:54.300
we always emphasize the key
role played by incentives.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.300
And we tend to think that by
giving material incentives

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.670
through markets or through institutions,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.950
we can affect individual behavior.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:09.000
And so, changing the
structure of incentives,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.270
is an underlying principle
for many of the interventions

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.690
we have including
anti-poverty interventions.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.810
If you look at other social sciences,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.450
and here, I mentioned
psychology, sociology,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:25.443
but in general, the focus there
when they analyze behavior,

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.670
includes many other factors,

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.460
for example, identity or social influence.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.383
And within these disciplines,

00:04:35.535 --> 00:04:38.850
norm-based interventions
are a lot more common.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.260
And in that case,

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.380
the understanding is that
you can change behavior

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.340
by shifting perception of what is normal,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.660
what is expected in one's reference group.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:52.920
So, there's no reason

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.950
why these two approaches
should be seen as distinct

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.210
or mutually exclusive, quite the opposite.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:03.390
In my research, I've tried
to understand the extent

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.930
to which they can compliment
and reinforce each other.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.270
So Rohini mentioned Kuznets,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:14.270
and if you read the motivation
for his Nobel Prize,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.240
we all associate his contributions
to growth, inequality.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.700
But the motivation actually mentions,

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.850
his empirically founded
interpretation of growth,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.950
which has led to a new and deepen insight

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:29.201
into the economic and social structure,

00:05:29.201 --> 00:05:31.140
and the process of development.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.810
So it is important, I think,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.900
also in the context of
this lecture to underline

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.780
that the importance of social factors,

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.430
has been there since
the origin of our field

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.110
and our profession.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:51.110
So I'm gonna give you a very
quick kind of definition

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:57.000
of norms borrowing on from the
work of Cristina Bicchieri,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.530
who's one of the leading
researchers on social norms.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.480
And Cristina defines norms
as shared behavioral rules

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.320
that prescribe or prescribe behaviors

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.600
within a large class of situations.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.480
So, we can think of norms
as a form of grammar.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.690
Grammar tells you how
you should arrange words,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.690
sentences, what is
acceptable, what is not,

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.090
in the way you are of speaking or writing.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.340
And norms instead of dictating

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.740
how words are formed and ordered,

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.530
dictates the behavior
that you are expected

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.130
to engage in particular situations.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:40.130
Now, these behaviors are
basically interdependent.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.520
And so the individual choice
to comply with the norm,

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.800
naturally depends on the expectations

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.140
of what other people will do.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:51.723
And there's two types of expectations,

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.100
one empirical or positive, we might think,

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.940
which is what do you
expect other people to do

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.320
in a similar situation?

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.070
So. how common is it to
choose this behavior?

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.350
Would a sufficiently large fraction

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.470
of my reference group take this action?

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.960
The other is the normative expectation.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.090
What should you do, okay?

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.670
What do other expect that you should do?

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.058
And in this case, the question becomes,

00:07:20.058 --> 00:07:24.540
is there a sufficiently large
fraction in the community

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:28.800
that expects me to behave
in a certain way, okay?

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.800
And these norms can really
play wonderful functions

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:40.380
or can become obstacles and be bad norms.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:45.380
So economists have studied the
benefits of norms in settings

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.480
where there are market imperfections,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.370
where some markets are
absent, institutions are also,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.610
you know, have limitations
when it comes to enforcement.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.420
And there's a lot of work
done on, for example,

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.420
the potential for informal
insurance networks

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.040
and or informal credit

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.820
and norms that govern cooperative behavior

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.860
within this type of transactions,

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.860
which obviously, have the
potential of facilitating growth.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.240
But today, we're gonna focus
on the dark side of norms,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.910
and of norms that might cause behavior

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.670
in a way that's inefficient,
not conducive to growth.

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:35.100
So the same type of risk sharing

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:40.100
that can function very
well to isolate the poor

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:42.450
from income fluctuations,

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.270
can also generate excessive pressure

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:50.040
or excessive solidarity,
as has been documented.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.810
Or there can be norms

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:56.810
that prescribe what
occupation you are expected

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:58.680
to engage in.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:02.490
For example, if you are a
member of a certain cast.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.970
We'll talk today about a norm
which has to do with gender.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:09.750
And unfortunately, in the area of gender,

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.330
you know, there's only an embarrassment

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:17.330
in how many of these
detrimental works you can find.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.780
Well, dowry, we know,

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.480
are payments associated with marriage

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.400
that impose very high costs often

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:32.970
in the order of a whole
year of income for a family.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.130
There's child marriage,

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.833
I'll talk about female genital cutting.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:43.760
Historically, there were norms
like food binding in China,

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.040
there are norms even to
this day, for example,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.460
that restrict women's
mobility during menstruation.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:54.750
All of these are norms that
for us are not so easy to come

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:58.830
to terms with because if we
measure the expected costs

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:02.280
and benefit in terms of productivity,

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:06.210
they don't seem to really play a role

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.550
that would justify their existence.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:12.600
So, having acknowledged this,

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:17.070
the question is gonna be why
are these norms still there?

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:22.070
And if we want to facilitate
their disappearance,

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.020
what can we do as policymakers?

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.060
And what I want to do today is

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:32.060
to take you through two sets
of results, considerations,

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:39.900
analytical work that I've engaged in,

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:43.560
one that looks at the evolution of norms

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:48.330
without a specific intervention
on behalf of policymakers,

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:52.350
and tries to understand to what extent

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:57.350
we can expect these norms
to fade away on the role.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.620
The second is a more hands-on approach

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.417
where you think about policy and you say,

00:11:04.417 --> 00:11:09.417
"Is there a role for
information provision?

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.680
Can we think that there's
some misperception

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.170
that is keeping these norms alive?

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.500
What is the role played by
culture or by identity?"

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.910
And here I will report on some recent work

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.270
that I've done with
actually some of the people

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.540
who are here in the audience,

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:35.540
and that sheds light using experiments.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.590
And in both cases,

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:42.270
I will use one of these
extremely harmful gender norms,

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:45.000
which is female genital cutting.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.120
Although I think, at
least for the first part,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:49.710
when we talk about evolution

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:53.760
and kind of the theoretical
analysis of these transitions,

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.320
I don't think there is anything

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:59.220
that is really necessarily specific of FGC

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.990
in what I will say, okay?

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:04.320
So, what is female genital cutting?

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.210
It is the practice of cutting

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.150
or removing part of the external

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:12.990
or internal female genitalia
for no medical reasons.

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.570
It's an initiation ritual that's performed

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:19.620
on girls between infancy and age 15.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:24.620
Very often it's around
eight to 11 years old

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:26.640
that girls are cut,

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.640
and it's again, in most cases
performed not in a hospital.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:36.780
So, there's some risk
associated with infection

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.780
and you know, all
traditional health practices.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.070
It's actually a widespread norm.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:50.940
There's 200 million girls
and women alive today

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.330
who have been cut in Africa,
Middle East and Asia.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:58.260
And once you have been cut,

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:01.380
there can be serious health consequences,

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.890
both at the time of
cutting, as I mentioned,

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:08.340
but even later on, increased risk of STIs,

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:11.730
difficulty of giving birth to children.

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:16.730
So, here you see a map of
Africa with a gradient in color

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:21.270
that shows you the
prevalence of FGC among women

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.460
in the age range 15 to 49.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:25.500
This is data from UNICEF.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.950
And you see, there are
countries like Somalia

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:32.943
where basically the practice is universal,

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:35.760
even countries like Egypt,

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:40.760
have had extremely high
rates estimated at 91%.

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:44.190
The other country I'll talk
about today is Sierra Leone.

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:45.480
Well, I won't talk about it,

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.220
I will just quickly reference it,

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.800
that where the practice is at 90%,

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:54.300
but you can see how there's
a very high prevalence

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:57.750
of the practice across Africa.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:02.750
So you know, the first question
that we can try and ask is

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.400
if we take a long-term view

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:10.680
of how this practice have evolved

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.323
and what's gonna happen in the future,

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:17.610
what do we have to say as economists.

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:21.000
And I think here,

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:26.000
it's helpful to understand
both modeling choices

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:28.630
that have been used and policy approaches

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:32.730
in the light of what's
been the common wisdom.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:37.380
The common wisdom is that with
something negative like FGC,

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:40.590
you go in and you say,
"Don't do it, okay?"

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:42.540
So the models are,

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:47.340
you have a choice of choosing
the detrimental action

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:48.900
or the other action.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:52.470
And very often, these prescriptions,

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:55.620
even when they translate
into law, are not respected.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.110
So, dowry is actually illegal,

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.450
but you know, it's not disappearing.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.490
And similarly for FGC,

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:05.400
many countries have
legislation against it.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:09.540
Now, what I'll try to do in
this first part is to say,

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:14.070
suppose that the goal is
to get rid of these norms

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:15.570
in the long run.

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:20.570
What happens if instead of
adopting a zero-one perspective,

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:22.710
you consider a setting

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.500
where there are intermediate alternatives.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.020
So something that is still bad

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.900
but a bit less bad than the extreme, okay?

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.750
And in the example of dowry,

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:36.000
you could say instead of outlawing dowry,

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.530
you could put a cap.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.270
In the example of child marriage,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:42.510
you could say, "Instead
of saying it's age 18,

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:47.510
let's decrease the minimum
age at marriage to say 16."

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:51.570
What would be the effect
of something like this?

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:54.150
And this is an interesting question

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:58.650
because of course, there
can be implications

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:02.340
that relate to human rights and values

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.550
on which I will be silent in this lecture,

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:10.550
but taking the kind of efficiency
perspective and saying,

00:16:12.097 --> 00:16:14.250
"What is it that's gonna make it easier

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.100
to get rid of the norm in the end?"

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.360
There is a trade-off.

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:20.490
So on the positive side,

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:22.740
you can think that there are individuals

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:23.910
who if you face them

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:27.420
with the choice between
a trait and nothing,

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.880
are still not willing
to abandon the trait.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:34.590
So they would stick with
the detrimental action.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:37.020
But if you propose a trait

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.960
that is to some extent a substitute,

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:42.240
they might take that step,

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.157
and then you go one
period ahead and you say,

00:16:45.157 --> 00:16:48.270
"Now, that you are at
this different trait,

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.850
maybe it's easier to take an extra step

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.230
and so on and so forth,

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:55.560
until you eventually get
rid of the normal together."

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:56.393
And this is what

00:16:56.393 --> 00:17:00.210
we're gonna call stepping
stone convergence.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.510
But on the negative side,

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:07.800
think again about having moved
from the extremely bad trait

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.750
to the intermediate one.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.870
What's your incentive to
abandon that intermediate trait

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:13.800
at that point?

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:16.770
Precisely because it's less costly,

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:19.650
you might actually decide
to stay there forever, okay?

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:22.710
We say that that becomes
an absorbing state.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:26.070
In that case, the presence
of the intermediate trait,

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:29.280
has not helped you,
quite the opposite, okay?

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:34.280
So, we came across these trade-off,

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:39.720
precisely when we were thinking
about policy work in Somalia

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:44.340
because as you will see
in Somalia with the Salim

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.840
and other cutters,

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.480
as you will see, there are different types

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:50.400
of female circumcision.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:53.610
And in our dialogues with policy partners

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.660
who were holding this
very dichotomic view,

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.490
it's bad and all forms,

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:02.280
should be really actively discouraged.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.230
We started thinking, you know,

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.480
would it make it easier for us to focus

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.420
on the most extreme as
opposed to the intermediate?

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:12.210
But then we also understood,
well, there are risks,

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:17.210
and we decided to try and
use some theory to guide us

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:19.860
in our understanding of these trade-offs.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:24.860
So, what this paper does is
to think about social norms

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:29.280
in a setting where is interdependence,

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.360
but where more than two
actions are allowed.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.030
We will study the transition dynamics

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.820
and we derive conditions under

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:43.650
which this type of stepping
stone convergence can occur.

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:47.070
And then we develop an
econometric approach

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:50.370
that allows us to test
whether this conditions hold

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:51.203
in the data.

00:18:51.203 --> 00:18:54.480
So, we collected some data in Somalia,

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:59.010
and basically, try to
map the model to the data

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:02.340
to see what can we say about
what happened in the past

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:03.930
and about the future.

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:08.370
And although as I will
show the Somalia case,

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:10.710
we think, it's particularly interesting.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.930
There's nothing in what I say,

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:15.180
especially, on the theoretical front

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:19.440
that really makes this study
of transition specific to FGC.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:22.260
So, why FGC?

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:24.720
Then as an example,

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.910
if you look at the WHO definition,

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:30.630
there are different types
of increasing severity.

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.700
They're called type I, II and III.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:35.940
And in Somalia, indeed there
are even different names

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:37.410
for these practices.

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:41.640
Types I and II, that are less
invasive are called Sunna,

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:46.053
and type III that's more
invasive, it's called Pharaonic.

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.890
And so going to the model,

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:54.890
I'm gonna sometimes use
the term Sunna or Pharaonic

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:57.240
to give you kind of a representation

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.400
of what these actions are,

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:01.770
but just think of this as a model

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:04.980
where there's a large population of agents

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:07.440
and they can choose between three actions,

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.080
low, medium, and high, okay?

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:14.280
I'm gonna rank them in this
way when we think about Sunna

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:18.570
because what I have in
mind is the health costs

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:19.980
of these actions.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:22.770
Of course, we can go back to this later.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:26.280
If you think about either
a religious component

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:27.990
or a cultural component,

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.780
it's not obvious that
they would be ordered

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.250
in this direction.

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:34.980
But then again, as you will see,

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:39.480
you know, the argument for
stepping stone can only exist

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.690
where there is at least some benefit

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:47.220
in moving from the intermediate
stage to the other stage.

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:50.010
If the intermediate action, Sunna,

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:54.660
had both a kind of a health
benefit and a cultural benefit

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:56.520
and dominated the other two,

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:59.400
no stepping stone transition can occur.

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.490
So at any point, there's
a proportion of players

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:04.320
that choose a certain action.

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.890
And what's key, and this
is a standard formulation

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:09.780
in the analysis of norms,

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:14.640
is that your utility from
a given action will depend

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.940
on the vector, which includes,

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.200
you know, what fraction

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.980
of the population has
chosen the various options?

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.620
And while you do have an intrinsic utility

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.880
that's independent from
what everyone else is doing,

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:33.780
there is this social
utility which is decreasing

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:37.260
in the share of people
who choose action, J,

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.570
different from Us, okay?

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:42.270
And how is it decreasing?

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:46.080
Well, most models, Brock
and Dulo and others,

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:50.190
use these social penalty
factors where someone

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:54.900
who chooses J will
inflict a cost, S, on U,

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:56.940
when you choose I different from J.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:01.890
So these S parameters are
assumed to be positive, okay?

00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:05.880
They are zero for those
who choose your own action.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:10.290
And so, this term is
what generates the desire

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.990
for conformity, okay?

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.450
And then there's an error term

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:20.313
that is assumed to be
extreme value distributed.

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:27.750
So, we're assuming the model, as I said,

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:31.920
we rank the utility and
we assume positive costs.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:36.090
Different from what most
of the literature is doing,

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:37.830
we're not assuming symmetry.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:42.090
So, we allow people who
choose I to punish those

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.100
who choose J in a different way

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:47.640
from how those who choose J
punish those who choose I,

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:52.640
which if you think again of
examples kind of make sense

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:54.810
because it's not obvious

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:56.970
that this punishment should be symmetric,

00:22:56.970 --> 00:22:58.830
this complicates the analysis

00:22:58.830 --> 00:23:03.830
because you can no longer
use potential games,

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:07.950
and proofs become a
little bit more complex.

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:12.950
But the key dynamics that
we identify are actually,

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:15.660
we are able to do it even

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:19.890
without the assumption of symmetry.

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:21.000
Time is continuous,

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:25.560
people receive revision
opportunities at random intervals.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:27.420
And so, on average,

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:31.200
you expect to have one
revision opportunity,

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:32.730
per unit of time.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.310
And agents because of these error term,

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:37.920
are gonna choose perturbed best responses.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:40.770
So, most of the time
they play best response,

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:42.990
but there are some deviations.

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:46.413
And with the extreme
value distributed shocks,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:48.840
it's a well-known result

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.840
that the probability of
shooting a certain action,

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:56.730
when the state is P is given
by this logic function.

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:01.730
And when the parameter beta is
the precision of the process.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:05.640
So, when beta is zero, you're
basically choosing at random.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:09.963
When beta tends to one, you're
choosing a best response.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:14.460
And in general, although
the dynamics are complex,

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.120
a lot of the intuition
can be gained by looking

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.400
at the case with the large population,

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:25.110
and with you know, that
approximate best responses.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:28.410
So, I'm gonna give you the
definition of stepping stone

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.660
and then the most important part

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:33.570
of the theoretical contribution,

00:24:33.570 --> 00:24:37.620
is understanding the
conditions for stepping stone

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.690
and the transition dynamics.

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:43.230
So an action, M, which remember,

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.730
is the intermediate one,

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:49.170
is a stepping stone if
along every dynamic path

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:50.880
in the large population limit.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:54.873
When you start from the norm,
that is the worst action,

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:58.350
you have that the fraction
of people choosing

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:00.750
that decreases over time.

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:03.420
The fraction choosing
the intermediate action,

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:05.760
first increases and then decreases.

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:10.760
And finally, the fraction
that choose the best "Action,"

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:15.000
increases until you
basically have the majority

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:18.570
of the population playing the high action.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:22.020
And the dynamics have
this interesting feature

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.970
that there is an inflection point.

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.670
So, what's the inflection
point is when basically,

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:30.210
you are indifferent between
playing the intermediate

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:32.040
and the high utility action,

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:35.827
that's when finally the
process will make the jump

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:40.590
to the best equilibrium.

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:45.590
And in this case, we can say
that if M is a stepping stone,

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:49.770
that the transition occurs
in the following way.

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:52.500
First people start from the bad norm

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:54.900
and they deviate to the intermediate one.

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:59.100
This is what this smaller
step is allowing you to do.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:01.800
At some point, there's gonna be enough

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:05.160
of a fraction of people that
are choosing the intermediate

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:07.860
that whoever deviates from then on,

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.950
can do the big jump directly, okay?

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:13.080
And that's a function of the fact

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:15.990
that by having reduced
the fraction of those

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:20.220
who are playing the very negative action,

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:22.830
the punishments are now decreased,

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:27.420
because the other group is now fragmented,

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:29.280
some play M, some play S.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.940
And so you don't get
punished by a big fraction

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:35.700
that's playing the L action, okay?

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:40.700
And this is the expression
for this inflection point

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:44.100
which we did not with q Star.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:47.063
So, if you think about the dynamics,

00:26:47.063 --> 00:26:49.050
when you have a stepping stone,

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:52.200
what happens is that you're
starting from an equilibrium

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.810
where you know, most of the population,

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:57.480
is playing the L action.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:01.230
I told you in Somalia,
98% of the women are cut.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:05.220
You will see that until
more or less the mid '90s,

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:09.180
that was actually 98%
being cut with Pharaonic,

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:10.410
the worst form.

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:12.240
So, we were here, okay?

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:15.480
And then you see a transition happening,

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:19.020
towards the intermediate,
which is Sunna, okay?

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.780
Now, this is kind of a theoretical graph.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:24.390
I'm not telling you that this
is what the data suggests,

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:27.660
but if in the case where
Sunna were stepping stone,

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:29.130
we would be seeing something

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:31.440
where the moment you approach q Star,

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:36.440
then the system moves
to the best equilibrium,

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:40.650
which is people actually
playing the uncut.

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:43.710
But if Sunna becomes absorbing,

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:45.570
at that point, you go here,

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:49.590
and then you get in the basin
of attraction of the M, norm,

00:27:49.590 --> 00:27:52.290
and you're no longer reaching the uncut,

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:55.863
unless you were already starting
quite close to that, okay?

00:27:56.730 --> 00:28:01.680
So, when is it that we can
expect this virtuous process

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.660
of convergence to occur?

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:06.060
The necessary and sufficient condition

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:10.830
in the best response model
are three inequalities.

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:15.300
So, the first inequality here
is the ratio of the difference

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:18.180
in social sanction when starting,

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.010
I'm gonna give you the Sunna example,

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:23.220
starting from Pharaonic,
you deviate one cut,

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:26.580
minus starting from Pharaonic,
you deviate to Sunna.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:30.300
And this is the difference
in intrinsic utility.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:34.800
So, having a relatively large
value of this ratio means

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:39.517
that the social cost
of switching from say,

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:44.640
worst action to the best one is too high,

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:47.280
relative to the gaining intrinsic utility

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:48.240
that you would get.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:52.740
So that's why you're not
doing the big jump directly.

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:55.080
What are the other two conditions say?

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.300
These conditions are saying that instead,

00:28:57.300 --> 00:28:59.520
if you compare the social sanctions

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:02.670
for going either from Pharaonic to Sunna

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:04.680
or from Sunna to uncut,

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:08.760
and you relate those to the
gains intrinsic utility,

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:12.120
this time the sanctions
are relatively low, okay?

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:15.333
So, that's why once you've
done the first step,

00:29:16.260 --> 00:29:18.270
this is why you can do the first step.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:20.640
And this is why once
you've done the first step,

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:24.120
you can then go to the
uncut equilibrium, okay?

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:27.630
So, you can rearrange this

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:31.110
and get that a necessary condition,

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:33.240
not sufficient, but necessary,

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:36.840
is the so-called reverse
triangle inequality,

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:38.730
which says that the sanction

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:42.330
for making the big step directly is

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:45.630
at least as large as the sum of the two.

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:49.320
And what's very intuitive
about this condition,

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.090
what's intuitive is that it tells you

00:29:51.090 --> 00:29:55.890
that stepping stones
existence is related to a form

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.980
of convexity in sanctions, okay?

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:01.530
So, when you get punished more harshly

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:06.420
for deviating a lot from
the current state of things,

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:10.230
it's a good idea to split it into pieces,

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:12.330
because the sanctions for each

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:15.390
of the steps is relatively lower, okay?

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:18.150
So, this is all quite intuitive.

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:22.020
So, let's take the case of Somalia,

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:27.020
and let's try first of all
to see how these two forms

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:32.640
of female circumcision
have evolved over time.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:34.890
So with Salim and other cutters,

00:30:34.890 --> 00:30:39.890
we collected data in 141
communities across Somalia,

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:44.220
sampling over 4,000
individuals, men and women,

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:47.405
that took part in community meetings

00:30:47.405 --> 00:30:52.290
for an experiment that
I will then describe.

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:56.640
And altogether, this
implies having information

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:59.670
on cutting for the female respondents

00:30:59.670 --> 00:31:04.500
and for the daughters who are
approximately 6,500 daughters.

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:06.780
And what we know is,

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:10.860
what type of cutting they
have when they were cut,

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:13.200
then we know attitudes towards cutting

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.113
as well as beliefs
about community support.

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:21.240
And a first thing that was
quite interesting is that

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:25.620
if you look at the incidence
of these two types,

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:28.890
Pharaonic, Sunna, across sub-clans,

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:32.910
you see that they're
pretty much both present,

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:35.280
which means that one type

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:38.520
or the other is not an
ethnic marker, okay?

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:43.380
So, it's not about identifying
one sub-clan versus another.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:46.980
You see that the share uncut
is in fact extremely low

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:48.690
in our data as well.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:50.280
The other thing that's interesting,

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:52.770
if instead of sub-clans,

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:55.680
you calculate these rates by communities.

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:59.430
So, these are 141 bars.

00:31:59.430 --> 00:32:02.250
You see that with the few exceptions

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:04.590
that are either entirely Sunna

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:06.420
or kind of entirely Pharaonic,

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:09.630
by and large, you see
prevalence of both forms

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:11.670
in each and every community.

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:13.620
And again, unfortunately,

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:18.620
not huge shares of uncut women
and girls anywhere, okay?

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:23.760
So descriptives, the age at

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:27.870
which girls are cut here
is around 8.5 or nine,

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:29.490
depending on the type,

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:33.390
and the decision to be
cut when we prompt them

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:35.340
and say, "Who makes this decision?"

00:32:35.340 --> 00:32:38.100
And you can say, "More than one person."

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:41.790
You see, indeed in most cases,

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:45.180
the mother is held
responsible for that, okay?

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:50.180
In about one-third to 40% of
the case also, the father,

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:53.250
grandmothers seem to play a lower role.

00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:55.710
Of course, this does not exclude the fact

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:59.010
that the mother might
internalize the preferences

00:32:59.010 --> 00:32:59.843
of the father.

00:32:59.843 --> 00:33:02.280
So you know, I'm not
making too much out of it,

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:06.150
but it's still something
that to some extent,

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:09.533
is implemented generation after generation

00:33:09.533 --> 00:33:14.533
with some element of
decision also by women.

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:16.897
And I told you,

00:33:16.897 --> 00:33:21.897
you know, there are
intrinsic utility payoffs

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:24.780
which can include many, many factors.

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:28.470
Here's the only one type of
factors that could go in there

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:30.660
and that's the health cost.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:34.020
But these health costs we
find are quite informative,

00:33:34.020 --> 00:33:36.600
because when you ask female respondents,

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:39.480
did you yourself experience complication?

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:43.500
You see that 63% almost
experienced complication

00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:48.500
from Pharaonic, and only 11% from Sunna.

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:50.880
If you go to the next generation,

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:52.320
which is the daughters,

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:55.230
Pharaonic complications
are still extremely high

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:58.950
because this is really,
extremely invasive.

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:01.530
Those from Sunna go down to 3%.

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:03.630
And then we have the breakdown

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:06.060
into what type of complication.

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:09.840
And you can see a very similar
pattern where from Pharaonic,

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:13.560
you have all sorts of
negative health complications,

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:17.190
but with Sunna, it's quite low, okay?

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:19.620
And I want you to keep this in mind,

00:34:19.620 --> 00:34:23.610
because you will see what conclusions

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:25.800
we reach about the stepping stone.

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:28.710
But intuitively, if you
are expecting people

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:32.250
to have incentives to abandon
the practice, for example,

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:35.640
because it's very costly
in terms of health,

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:39.960
this doesn't seem to be in
place from their understanding

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:41.793
or their experience, okay?

00:34:42.720 --> 00:34:47.720
So, let's try and use
the model to shed light

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:51.060
on what the transition process looks like.

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.100
So, we need to map into the model,

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:57.180
we need approximations for
the stock and the flow, okay?

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:01.590
And what we are gonna do in the analysis

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:04.080
that I show you is to say,

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:07.058
remember the cutting age around nine.

00:35:07.058 --> 00:35:10.383
I'm gonna say that if you are 10 or more,

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:15.630
and you have a certain trait,
you enter that stock, okay?

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:19.200
If you are 10 or more
and you are still uncut,

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:22.237
but when we prompt your parents, you say,

00:35:22.237 --> 00:35:25.440
"Okay, your daughter is uncut,
what are you planning to do?"

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:27.067
And they have an option of say,

00:35:27.067 --> 00:35:30.480
"You know, cut her with
this type of circumcision

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:31.770
or leave her uncut."

00:35:31.770 --> 00:35:34.410
If they say that you're gonna be cut,

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:36.150
it's just a matter of time,

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:38.580
and they give the type of circumcision,

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:42.510
we actually attribute
that girl to the stock,

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:45.600
because in our model, you
get to choose once, okay?

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:48.060
So we are trying to map this to the model

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:51.660
and this also helps because at
that point, if you are uncut,

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:53.640
there's no single point in time

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:56.970
where we could pinpoint
that you would be uncut.

00:35:56.970 --> 00:35:59.580
And this way, everybody it gets 10,

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:03.150
gets assigned to whatever
the final action will be.

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:07.830
But in the paper, we use other
forms of computing stocks

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:09.660
and flows and the those are similar.

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:12.150
So the stock is gonna be basically,

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:16.140
the cumulative proportions
for the cohorts we have.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:19.290
And the flow is, in any year,

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:21.390
you know, how many girls are cut

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:24.450
with a certain type of cut, okay?

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:26.970
And we use three year moving average

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:29.010
to get rid of some of the noise.

00:36:29.010 --> 00:36:31.350
And you know, these results are robust

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:35.490
to instead of 10 using
cut-offs of nine, 10 or 12.

00:36:35.490 --> 00:36:38.800
So here is the evolution of the flow

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:44.720
from the early '70s, until when
we collected the data, okay?

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:50.490
So, the blue line is giving
you the flow of Pharaonic cuts.

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:55.490
So that means that the women
who were cut in these decades,

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:59.370
were pretty much always
cut Pharaonic, okay?

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:03.030
There's a slow increase
in the share Sunna,

00:37:03.030 --> 00:37:05.610
until the early to mid '90s

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:10.050
where you see that suddenly
things start speeding up,

00:37:10.050 --> 00:37:15.050
and then Sunna is now the
prevalent form of cutting, okay?

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:18.360
Everybody, pretty much most of the people

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:21.270
who get cut today, are cut with Sunna.

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:24.090
There are still some
girls cut with Pharaonic.

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:26.790
You see uncut is quite stable and low.

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:28.440
There's this little blip here,

00:37:28.440 --> 00:37:31.080
but really almost unnoticeable.

00:37:31.080 --> 00:37:34.200
So if you think about norms transition,

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:37.320
we find this to be a pretty striking graph

00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:42.320
because it really shows one
or being displaced by another.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.460
Of course, when you do the stocks

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:48.210
because there's the early flows contribute

00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:53.210
to generating a cumulative
share of ironic that stays high,

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:55.860
of course, you know,

00:37:55.860 --> 00:37:58.200
the crossing occurs a bit later,

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:01.680
but already these days the stock

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:05.370
of Sunna is now higher
than that of Pharaonic.

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:09.390
Okay, so how can we explain that blip?

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:10.860
So, if you think of the model,

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:14.160
what the model is saying
is that you're starting

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:15.990
from a bad place, PL,

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:18.540
that's where we were
with a lot of Pharaonic.

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:22.140
And then you are gonna
have some of these error

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:26.146
that you know generates
some people choosing Sunna.

00:38:26.146 --> 00:38:29.850
As that share grows,

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:34.850
at some point, it reaches
a tilting point, okay?

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:38.010
So basically, the model
could endogenously,

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:40.533
generate the type of transition,

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:45.060
through the increasing
accumulation of Sunna.

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:49.967
If you read historical accounts
of what happened in Somalia,

00:38:49.967 --> 00:38:54.210
it's also true that since the mid '80s,

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:57.300
human rights organization were very vocal,

00:38:57.300 --> 00:39:00.300
especially, against
Pharaonic circumcision,

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:03.360
and eventually, religious leaders decided

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:07.380
to distant themselves from
Pharaonic and started emphasizes

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:10.860
that this was not a requirement of Islam.

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.050
So you can also think of this

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:17.460
as kind of reinforcing the
potential dynamics of the model Y

00:39:17.460 --> 00:39:20.550
because you can think that the fact

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:24.270
that the Imams were now
endorsing Sunna relative

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:28.200
to Pharaonic is gonna reduce the sanctions

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:30.330
for going from Pharaonic to Sunna.

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:33.990
Also, you know, it's gonna
increase the benefits of Sunna

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:35.880
if you are a religious person.

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:36.930
And at the same time,

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:39.390
all of these campaigns against Pharaonic,

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:43.380
decrease the relative
utility of Pharaonic.

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:48.060
So, all of these can help
us look at what happened

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:51.120
in the past, make sense of it.

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:54.570
But of course, the
question we have now is,

00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:56.850
given that everybody's cutting Sunna,

00:39:56.850 --> 00:39:59.160
is this just the first stage

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:04.160
of a transition out of
FGC completely or not?

00:40:04.170 --> 00:40:06.630
And here, ideally you would want

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:10.380
to have estimates of
those U and S parameters,

00:40:10.380 --> 00:40:13.159
which are quite difficult to have,

00:40:13.159 --> 00:40:16.710
and we try to have some
survey-based proxies,

00:40:16.710 --> 00:40:19.860
which we include in the paper

00:40:19.860 --> 00:40:22.410
to corroborate the other approach.

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:24.960
But today, I will not
show you those results.

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:26.700
But what's interesting is

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:30.330
that in the absence of
those parameters estimates,

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:33.660
you can still use some
of the properties implied

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:37.830
by the model and the
estimates of stocks and flows,

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:41.490
and that's enough to predict
what's gonna happen, okay?

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:43.560
So, I'm gonna show you three tests

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:48.067
that can be used in general,
not just for this FGC approach,

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:54.120
to understand if a certain
action can be a stepping stone.

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:57.600
So, the first comes from the
reverse triangle inequality.

00:40:57.600 --> 00:41:00.690
Remember, we said that's
a necessary condition,

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:05.370
and with this logic
specification that we have,

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:09.330
you can express the log ratio of the flows

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:14.330
as a function of utility and
then the different stocks.

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:20.160
And basically, if you
write it for the flow

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:23.820
of uncut over Sunna, you find,

00:41:23.820 --> 00:41:25.980
sorry this is a P that's missing,

00:41:25.980 --> 00:41:29.850
you find that the coefficient
on the stock of Pharaonic,

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:33.300
is actually given by a positive constant

00:41:33.300 --> 00:41:36.660
in the model multiplied by this, okay?

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:39.000
What is this expression?

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:41.820
It's basically the reverse
triangle inequality,

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:44.250
because remember, that
inequality was saying

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:46.920
that, "SLH needs to be at least

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:49.410
as large as the sum of these two."

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:52.380
So how can you test whether
the necessary condition

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:53.970
for stepping stone is met?

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:58.770
You can run a regression of
these log ratio of the flows

00:41:58.770 --> 00:42:01.260
on the two stocks, PH and PL,

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:03.360
and just look at whether the coefficient

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:06.153
on PL is negative or not, okay?

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:09.030
And when you run that regression,

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:12.750
you actually find a positive,
not a negative coefficient,

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:15.847
which is a first way of saying,

00:42:15.847 --> 00:42:19.380
"The necessary condition for
stepping stone does not hold

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:20.310
in the data."

00:42:20.310 --> 00:42:24.780
So this is test number one, it's nice,

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:26.610
it does rely on the fact

00:42:26.610 --> 00:42:29.760
that this specific functional form comes

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:32.460
from the logic formulation.

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:36.180
Second test is what we
call equal flow test.

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:39.570
This one does not rely
on the logic formulation.

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:40.860
What is an equal flow?

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:44.340
Remember, the inflection point that says

00:42:44.340 --> 00:42:46.860
that, "At some point, you are gonna have

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:51.180
that the utility from
the two choices is equal

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:53.790
if you are on your path to a transition."

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:58.620
In particular, you can
derive the condition that

00:42:58.620 --> 00:43:01.950
if at some point you
have a decreasing share

00:43:01.950 --> 00:43:05.940
of say, Pharaonic, and
the stock of uncut is

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:09.150
at least as large as that of Sunna,

00:43:09.150 --> 00:43:11.910
from that point on,
the system is predicted

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:13.830
to converge to uncut, okay?

00:43:13.830 --> 00:43:16.650
So, this is what would be happening.

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:18.330
This is the equal flow,

00:43:18.330 --> 00:43:23.310
means that at some point,
the flow of the two is equal,

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:26.310
and from that point, you
definitely go to uncut.

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:27.330
So, what have we done?

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:29.880
We have computed these sigmas,

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:34.170
flow into uncut and flow
into Sunna for every year

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:35.610
in our data.

00:43:35.610 --> 00:43:39.870
And sorry, let me skip this.

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:43.440
And we have plotted the frequency

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:48.440
of how many times was this
ratio of sigma H to sigma N,

00:43:48.456 --> 00:43:49.593
zero, 0.5, 0.1,

00:43:51.060 --> 00:43:53.370
I mean approximated in bins.

00:43:53.370 --> 00:43:56.850
Remember, if you say equal
flow and then you're off,

00:43:56.850 --> 00:44:00.660
it means that the ratio
should be 0.5 or larger.

00:44:00.660 --> 00:44:02.850
And you see that in our data,

00:44:02.850 --> 00:44:05.700
this ratio is always to the left, okay?

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:08.970
So, it's a sufficient,
not a necessary condition,

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:12.030
but we haven't reached
that sufficient condition

00:44:12.030 --> 00:44:14.100
in the data that we have so far.

00:44:14.100 --> 00:44:18.480
So, that's a further
confirmation that test one

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.370
and test two don't tell us

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.610
that we are on our path out of Sunna.

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:27.930
The last test that we do is to
basically simulate the model.

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:29.280
So, what we're doing is

00:44:29.280 --> 00:44:34.170
we are estimating the implied dynamics.

00:44:34.170 --> 00:44:36.360
And again, given our model,

00:44:36.360 --> 00:44:39.120
we don't need to estimate
all the parameters.

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:42.210
It's enough to estimate
these log odds ratios

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:44.850
as a function of the stocks.

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:48.180
And because you know, the flow sum at one,

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:49.440
basically, through this,

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:53.070
we can infer the mapping, sigma of P.

00:44:53.070 --> 00:44:55.803
So, when you simulate the model,

00:44:56.640 --> 00:45:01.410
this is the part for which we
actually have the data, okay?

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:03.630
This is what's been happening so far,

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:07.530
starting from Pharaonic, the
share of Pharaonic goes down,

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:09.000
the share of Sunna goes up.

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:12.240
You remember that uncut was
staying very close to zero.

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:15.840
This is what we're getting from
the model simulation, okay?

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:20.640
And this dotted part is the
extrapolation to future years,

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:23.700
and it's kind of unequivocally telling us

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:27.150
that we're gonna end up with
Sunna being absorbing, okay?

00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:29.613
So that's the third piece of evidence.

00:45:33.870 --> 00:45:37.560
It's not super uplifting as a conclusion.

00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:42.560
The truth is that we still
have relatively few years

00:45:42.810 --> 00:45:47.810
in which the flow into uncut
has been significant enough.

00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:52.620
And it's possible that if
something change, so for example,

00:45:52.620 --> 00:45:55.900
here we are simulating an exogenous shift

00:45:56.970 --> 00:45:59.490
that increases by 5%,

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:03.060
how many people are
choosing uncut over Sunna?

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:06.750
Actually, with that kind size of a shift,

00:46:06.750 --> 00:46:09.630
the simulation predicts
that you could get out.

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:12.810
So, I think there's some room for saying

00:46:12.810 --> 00:46:14.760
that maybe with more years of data,

00:46:14.760 --> 00:46:18.060
and if something happened that
kind of increased this talk

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:21.510
of uncut, aside from the
model dynamics per se,

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:23.820
we could get out of it, okay?

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:25.353
Now, I want to use,

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:30.570
so to sum up, if you
think of this framework,

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:34.320
you could try to facilitate the transition

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:36.120
by playing with those parameters

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:38.190
that govern the inequalities.

00:46:38.190 --> 00:46:41.940
So you could try to have
sanctions being reduced

00:46:41.940 --> 00:46:46.940
or you know, increase knowledge
about the relative benefits

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:54.500
or what kind of identity
payoffs people attached to this.

00:46:57.000 --> 00:46:58.980
I want to just emphasize

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:01.290
that while I've discussed everything

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:04.470
in terms of this FGC example,

00:47:04.470 --> 00:47:09.470
there are both historical and
contemporary examples of norms

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:11.430
where these intermediate action,

00:47:11.430 --> 00:47:16.050
can actually be pretty meaningful.

00:47:16.050 --> 00:47:17.640
One historical example

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:22.640
that my co-authors are
very fond of is dueling.

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:27.810
And in Europe, dueling
persisted for quite a long time,

00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:32.800
and it persisted longer
in France than in England.

00:47:33.720 --> 00:47:35.820
And one of the reasons is apparently

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:40.820
that they accepted a less
lethal form of weapon,

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:44.580
which is like the intermediate
action becoming absorbing

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:46.050
in a sense.

00:47:46.050 --> 00:47:51.050
If we think more kind of recent
technological innovation,

00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:53.220
let's think of smoking

00:47:53.220 --> 00:47:55.950
and the introduction of
electronic cigarettes,

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:58.920
this could be a way to help smokers quit

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:02.820
or because the damage is
less with e-cigarettes than

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:03.930
with regular cigarettes,

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:05.580
it might actually lead people not

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:07.770
to quit smoking altogether.

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:10.320
So I'm told I have 10 minutes,

00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:13.410
which doesn't leave me too much to talk

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:15.360
about policies to change FGC,

00:48:15.360 --> 00:48:18.540
but I'll try to give
you a flavors at least.

00:48:18.540 --> 00:48:23.347
So, if you now think of, let me take

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:30.120
where the head of policymaker,
be it the government and NGO,

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:34.350
who wants to help this
transition out of a harmful norm.

00:48:34.350 --> 00:48:35.820
What can I do?

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:39.240
Well, one thing is that
maybe I don't trust people

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:41.370
to have all the information they need

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:43.800
to assess this intrinsic utility.

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:46.260
So, I could do information campaigns.

00:48:46.260 --> 00:48:49.050
Maybe there's something that people attach

00:48:49.050 --> 00:48:51.450
to these practices that defines

00:48:51.450 --> 00:48:55.440
how they see themselves
and identity component.

00:48:55.440 --> 00:48:57.667
And here you could think,

00:48:57.667 --> 00:49:00.300
"Does the identity value really depend

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:02.790
on the harmful side of
the practice or not?"

00:49:02.790 --> 00:49:06.330
And then there are potential explanations

00:49:06.330 --> 00:49:09.390
that have more to do
with the part of the norm

00:49:09.390 --> 00:49:10.740
that's about the expectation.

00:49:10.740 --> 00:49:13.770
So for example, maybe people really think

00:49:13.770 --> 00:49:16.260
that everybody's in favor of cutting,

00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:19.620
but in private, they are not necessarily.

00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:22.080
And that's a form of
pluralistic ignorance.

00:49:22.080 --> 00:49:23.790
In that case, what you want to do is,

00:49:23.790 --> 00:49:28.790
realign people's beliefs or
maybe everybody has the exact,

00:49:28.860 --> 00:49:31.710
you know, rational beliefs
that they should have.

00:49:31.710 --> 00:49:34.230
Simply nobody wants to move first, okay?

00:49:34.230 --> 00:49:36.780
So, there's a simple coordination failure,

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:40.020
and in that case, can you
use coordination tools?

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:42.150
And some NGOs, for example,

00:49:42.150 --> 00:49:45.390
Tostan have used these public
declarations when you talk

00:49:45.390 --> 00:49:47.550
about FGC, you know,

00:49:47.550 --> 00:49:51.330
working with villages to have
a critical mass of families

00:49:51.330 --> 00:49:53.700
who pledge not to cut their daughters

00:49:53.700 --> 00:49:57.300
or not to marry their
sons, to cut daughters.

00:49:57.300 --> 00:50:01.350
So tomorrow, Lucia will present some work

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:03.810
that we've done in Sierra Leone

00:50:03.810 --> 00:50:08.310
where FGC is actually part
of an initiation ritual

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:09.330
that's called bondo.

00:50:09.330 --> 00:50:14.330
This is a group ceremony
that stretches over one month

00:50:14.520 --> 00:50:17.610
in the sense that the girls
go together in the bush,

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:21.180
they are taught how to
become mothers, wives.

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:25.680
They really create a
mutual network of support.

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:28.500
And at the conclusion of this month,

00:50:28.500 --> 00:50:30.690
the cutting ritual is done.

00:50:30.690 --> 00:50:34.347
And we've worked with the local NGO

00:50:34.347 --> 00:50:39.347
to test the effectiveness of
two potential interventions.

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:43.770
One is information about
the health costs of FGC,

00:50:43.770 --> 00:50:46.590
and the other is actually to try

00:50:46.590 --> 00:50:50.970
and discuss with
communities, the possibility

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:55.530
of using a ritual or choosing a ritual

00:50:55.530 --> 00:50:58.500
that includes everything
else that's there,

00:50:58.500 --> 00:51:01.710
but replaces the cutting
with an alternative sign.

00:51:01.710 --> 00:51:04.470
So, preserving in some way,

00:51:04.470 --> 00:51:07.500
the symbolic value of the practice.

00:51:07.500 --> 00:51:12.500
And if you want to know what
happens, show up tomorrow.

00:51:13.530 --> 00:51:16.380
Now, what I want to instead
maybe say a little bit more

00:51:16.380 --> 00:51:20.160
about is another intervention

00:51:20.160 --> 00:51:22.440
that we did with the
subset of the quarters

00:51:22.440 --> 00:51:26.220
of the Somalia transitions paper.

00:51:26.220 --> 00:51:30.570
And this is about
beliefs and coordination.

00:51:30.570 --> 00:51:32.850
So here, we're gonna tackle this idea

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:36.510
that maybe people are
privately against FGC,

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:41.510
but you know, they misperceive
other people's support for it

00:51:42.300 --> 00:51:45.150
or there is this coordination failure.

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:49.770
So, what we've done there is
to hold community meetings

00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:53.977
where, sorry, we gather
people in a group of

00:51:57.510 --> 00:52:00.960
about 30 participants in rural villages,

00:52:00.960 --> 00:52:04.200
and they were given a secret ballots

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:06.240
where they could say yes or no,

00:52:06.240 --> 00:52:09.540
if they wanted to abolish
Pharaonic circumcision.

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:12.870
So, there was no way to identify

00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:15.750
who was voting in a certain way.

00:52:15.750 --> 00:52:17.790
And once they cast their vote,

00:52:17.790 --> 00:52:20.910
we incentivize everybody to guess

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:23.370
what the number of people

00:52:23.370 --> 00:52:27.000
in support of abandoning
Pharaonic circumcision,

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:28.440
would've been, okay?

00:52:28.440 --> 00:52:31.260
Then in some of the
communities, we end the meeting.

00:52:31.260 --> 00:52:32.400
In these communities

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:35.640
what we will have is the
private support for the practice

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:39.690
and the beliefs about the
community level support.

00:52:39.690 --> 00:52:43.920
In one group of communities,
we open the ballot box,

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:47.310
and we say, "Here is
how many actually voted

00:52:47.310 --> 00:52:48.630
in favor of my abandoning."

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:52.500
So, this is the case
where you are correcting

00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:54.900
if there are misperceptions,

00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:58.740
you are realigning beliefs
with the actual preferences.

00:52:58.740 --> 00:53:00.930
And then we thought, you know,

00:53:00.930 --> 00:53:04.740
it's important to think that
aside from the beliefs part,

00:53:04.740 --> 00:53:08.370
there might be this coordination problems.

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:12.417
So, we try to design the
lab in the field equivalent

00:53:12.417 --> 00:53:15.540
of this public declarations.

00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:17.580
In some communities,

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:21.450
the people who was running
the meeting drew a circle

00:53:21.450 --> 00:53:23.887
on the floor and said,

00:53:23.887 --> 00:53:27.660
"If you are in favor of
abolishing Pharaonic circumcision,

00:53:27.660 --> 00:53:29.550
you can step into the circle."

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.370
So, this is gonna be our
coordination treatment.

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:34.680
And then there's a third
group of communities

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:38.220
where we first reveal the sharing support

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:42.450
and then we ask who wants
to step into the circle.

00:53:42.450 --> 00:53:45.330
Okay, we have three
rounds of data collection

00:53:45.330 --> 00:53:48.000
from 2018 to '21,

00:53:48.000 --> 00:53:51.630
and there's data on
traditional circumcisors

00:53:51.630 --> 00:53:54.450
that we also have and
I will not discuss now.

00:53:54.450 --> 00:53:57.600
So first, I'll just show you this graph

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:02.600
which has evidence that there
is indeed a significant extent

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:05.670
of misperception, why?

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:09.330
Because here you have the share of people

00:54:09.330 --> 00:54:13.020
who support the abandonment
of Pharaonic vote,

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:17.460
you know, from zero to 100%
of the meeting participants.

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:22.460
And the green dash line is the
actual vote, anonymous vote.

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:26.850
So, we have no reason
to think that they lied.

00:54:26.850 --> 00:54:30.900
This is telling us that in the
vast majority of communities,

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:35.010
the share is greater than 60%
with a very large fraction

00:54:35.010 --> 00:54:38.730
where it's almost close to 100%.

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:41.670
But the respondents prior about

00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:46.560
how many are in favor of
abandoning is shifted to the left,

00:54:46.560 --> 00:54:49.980
which means that they
actually expect less support,

00:54:49.980 --> 00:54:52.050
than there is in practice, okay?

00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:55.920
So this opens the possibility

00:54:55.920 --> 00:54:59.160
for correcting these misperceived beliefs.

00:54:59.160 --> 00:55:01.950
And similarly, if you
think about coordinator,

00:55:01.950 --> 00:55:03.450
this is the same thing,

00:55:03.450 --> 00:55:08.040
which is the guess about those who are

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:10.770
in favor of abandoning.

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:12.635
So, this is the belief,

00:55:12.635 --> 00:55:14.130
and these are the number of people

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:17.250
who ended up entering the circle, okay?

00:55:17.250 --> 00:55:21.300
So this exercise led more people to step

00:55:21.300 --> 00:55:24.240
into the circle than what they expected

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:28.200
to be the public support for the practice.

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:31.980
And I'll skip this one and
just go quickly to the result

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:34.050
because I'm almost out of time.

00:55:34.050 --> 00:55:36.120
So first of all, we go there,

00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:37.650
this is endline data.

00:55:37.650 --> 00:55:40.080
So, it's two years after the intervention,

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:44.820
and this is the share of girls

00:55:44.820 --> 00:55:47.790
who've been Pharaonic cut, okay?

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:52.790
So you know, the meaning the
control group is quite low

00:55:55.050 --> 00:55:56.460
because I already told you

00:55:56.460 --> 00:55:59.370
that nowadays most people cut Sunna.

00:55:59.370 --> 00:56:02.700
You could say, "Why did you
do the experiment on Pharaonic

00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:04.710
and not on Sunna then?"

00:56:04.710 --> 00:56:06.240
Because in our pilot,

00:56:06.240 --> 00:56:09.510
we understood that the support
for Sunna was quite high.

00:56:09.510 --> 00:56:10.890
So if you do one of these,

00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:13.627
open the ballot experiments
and then you say,

00:56:13.627 --> 00:56:17.970
"You know, 95% of the people
are supporting Sunna,"

00:56:17.970 --> 00:56:22.970
you're not gonna have much of
a a decrease in cutting rates.

00:56:23.250 --> 00:56:27.180
And at the same time, we
also did feel that given

00:56:27.180 --> 00:56:30.720
how much more harmful these practice is,

00:56:30.720 --> 00:56:35.720
even going from 4% to a lower
number was an important goal.

00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:40.920
And in fact, we managed to
reduce it by 1.6% points.

00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:45.900
Now, what we were not sure would happen

00:56:45.900 --> 00:56:50.310
and happened or you know,
could have happened or not,

00:56:50.310 --> 00:56:54.720
and instead happened was that
in this span of two years,

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:57.480
the rate of Sunna cuts actually went up,

00:56:57.480 --> 00:57:01.950
and the share of girls who
was uncut went down, okay?

00:57:01.950 --> 00:57:06.360
So, having seen that model
with the intermediate action,

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:08.910
this is not so surprising
because something

00:57:08.910 --> 00:57:13.910
that can happen is that in fact,
you go away from Pharaonic,

00:57:14.790 --> 00:57:16.620
you make the step to Sunna.

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:19.050
And now, that Sunna
becomes more appealing,

00:57:19.050 --> 00:57:22.230
there might be some people who from uncut,

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:24.780
are induced to actually choose the Sunna.

00:57:24.780 --> 00:57:28.680
So, this would not be a
very desirable outcome.

00:57:28.680 --> 00:57:30.180
However, it's also true

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:32.970
that these two years
after the intervention,

00:57:32.970 --> 00:57:34.890
is not giving you the steady state

00:57:34.890 --> 00:57:39.300
because girls get cut in
a certain age interval.

00:57:39.300 --> 00:57:43.110
So, if we want to know what
this does to parents preferences

00:57:43.110 --> 00:57:46.770
and to parents' choices for
all the girls that they have,

00:57:46.770 --> 00:57:50.910
including those who are
too young to be cut,

00:57:50.910 --> 00:57:55.910
we should factor in the preferences
about uncut girls, okay?

00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:58.830
So, in this table,

00:57:58.830 --> 00:58:01.080
you have the old result probability

00:58:01.080 --> 00:58:02.790
of being uncut goes down.

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:06.120
But then we look at
whether for those uncut,

00:58:06.120 --> 00:58:07.237
the parents say that,

00:58:07.237 --> 00:58:09.240
"They're planned to be cut in the future

00:58:09.240 --> 00:58:11.460
or planned not to be cut, okay?"

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:15.180
And what's happening is that
this number masks a reduction

00:58:15.180 --> 00:58:17.760
in those who are planned
to be cut in the future,

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:21.270
and an increase in those who
for the future are planned not

00:58:21.270 --> 00:58:22.380
to be cut.

00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:26.850
And indeed, if you look
at the age of cutting,

00:58:26.850 --> 00:58:28.680
it goes down a little bit,

00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:30.300
and sorry, I didn't say it before,

00:58:30.300 --> 00:58:33.570
but everything we have
in terms of results,

00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:37.620
is pretty much driven by the
misperceptions intervention.

00:58:37.620 --> 00:58:40.556
It turns out that our coordination device,

00:58:40.556 --> 00:58:43.920
didn't work as well.

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:47.580
And so, in fact, the age of
cutting went down a little bit,

00:58:47.580 --> 00:58:49.620
which means that that increase in Sunna

00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:52.590
that we saw is probably, you know,

00:58:52.590 --> 00:58:55.560
some girls whose cut wasn't anticipated.

00:58:55.560 --> 00:58:57.360
It was done a Sunna.

00:58:57.360 --> 00:59:01.080
And then if we take the
long-term perspective and say,

00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:02.760
now, let's take everybody,

00:59:02.760 --> 00:59:06.570
and if you are Pharaonic
cut now or plan to be,

00:59:06.570 --> 00:59:08.070
we give you a one.

00:59:08.070 --> 00:59:10.710
Sunna cut or plan to be an uncut

00:59:10.710 --> 00:59:12.630
and plan to stay uncut.

00:59:12.630 --> 00:59:17.630
If you take all of this,
the endpoint seems to be

00:59:18.330 --> 00:59:21.960
that there's actually
an increase in the share

00:59:21.960 --> 00:59:24.270
that in the long-term is gonna be uncut.

00:59:24.270 --> 00:59:26.010
Of course, with these plans,

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:28.140
we could say maybe it's cheap talk,

00:59:28.140 --> 00:59:31.628
we don't know what the parents
are gonna do in the end.

00:59:31.628 --> 00:59:35.529
So, the only thing we can do here is,

00:59:35.529 --> 00:59:38.970
because we had the
midline and the endline,

00:59:38.970 --> 00:59:42.840
we compared the plans that
they gave us at midline

00:59:42.840 --> 00:59:46.020
with the action that
they declare it endline

00:59:46.020 --> 00:59:49.650
in the age range in which
the girls are most at risk.

00:59:49.650 --> 00:59:54.650
And we have 532 girls of which basically,

00:59:55.230 --> 01:00:00.230
39 have been cut when the
parents at midline had said,

01:00:00.727 --> 01:00:02.850
"She's gonna stay uncut, okay?"

01:00:02.850 --> 01:00:06.900
So we have a 7% deviation from intentions,

01:00:06.900 --> 01:00:09.510
which again, you know,

01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:14.510
eventually, doesn't seem a
hugely important problem.

01:00:15.990 --> 01:00:20.990
But it's also true that
this is all we have

01:00:21.570 --> 01:00:23.250
in terms of the data.

01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:27.693
So, I'll skip the
additional results and just,

01:00:29.790 --> 01:00:33.930
I think just say that in the
context that we've studied,

01:00:33.930 --> 01:00:37.620
pluralistic ignorance
seems to play a role.

01:00:37.620 --> 01:00:41.040
And so in that case, simple intervention,

01:00:41.040 --> 01:00:45.180
based on correcting
misplaced beliefs is viable.

01:00:45.180 --> 01:00:50.180
The coordination exercise
seemed less effective and that,

01:00:50.451 --> 01:00:54.000
you know, disregarding this role

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:57.480
of intermediate roles is
actually a limitation,

01:00:57.480 --> 01:00:59.490
because we saw that in the short run,

01:00:59.490 --> 01:01:03.150
at least, some of what
happens was an increase

01:01:03.150 --> 01:01:04.950
of the intermediate form.

01:01:04.950 --> 01:01:09.240
So, there's something that
I have completely left out

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:14.240
of this discussion and we
had a very interesting,

01:01:14.490 --> 01:01:18.300
for me, conversation with
Rohini and Sam today,

01:01:18.300 --> 01:01:21.510
which is actually the role of the law,

01:01:21.510 --> 01:01:25.140
and of the institutions,
because all I've tried

01:01:25.140 --> 01:01:28.110
to discuss is still informally enforced.

01:01:28.110 --> 01:01:29.730
It's still a way

01:01:29.730 --> 01:01:33.990
of having the kind of
the social norm aspect,

01:01:33.990 --> 01:01:38.880
not sanctioned by an
authority, a legal authority.

01:01:38.880 --> 01:01:43.650
I think, it's very important
to also pay attention

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:48.650
to how legal and institutional solutions

01:01:52.890 --> 01:01:57.180
to norms change can be implemented
when they are successful,

01:01:57.180 --> 01:02:00.030
were not, and also I think,

01:02:00.030 --> 01:02:04.980
a longstanding question is
eventually you want people

01:02:04.980 --> 01:02:07.800
to own these changes.

01:02:07.800 --> 01:02:09.450
This means that their attitudes,

01:02:09.450 --> 01:02:12.750
towards harmful norms need to change.

01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:14.403
So, what comes first?

01:02:16.020 --> 01:02:19.650
Will my attitudes change because
I see a different behavior

01:02:19.650 --> 01:02:23.730
or is a different behavior
generated by attitudinal change?

01:02:23.730 --> 01:02:26.400
And I haven't found a way

01:02:26.400 --> 01:02:29.760
of solving this obvious
endogeneity problem,

01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:33.000
but for me, given this type of questions,

01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:38.000
it's an obvious important
future avenue for more work.

01:02:40.350 --> 01:02:42.605
So, thank you for your patience

01:02:42.605 --> 01:02:45.456
with me going slightly over time.

01:02:45.456 --> 01:02:48.623
(audience applauding)

01:02:55.399 --> 01:02:56.811
- [Rohini] I think we now have time

01:02:56.811 --> 01:03:00.728
for questions, so (indistinct)

01:03:04.350 --> 01:03:05.190
- [Man] Thank you.

01:03:05.190 --> 01:03:08.029
Thank you very much,
Eliana, for visiting us.

01:03:08.029 --> 01:03:10.650
- [Man] For visiting the center.

01:03:10.650 --> 01:03:13.233
(all laughing)

01:03:15.455 --> 01:03:18.450
- [Man] So, I wanted to
just connect the three parts

01:03:18.450 --> 01:03:19.283
of your presentation.

01:03:19.283 --> 01:03:21.180
I really like the experiment
you just described

01:03:21.180 --> 01:03:23.790
in Sierra Leone, which is, you know,

01:03:23.790 --> 01:03:25.533
beliefs about what other people,

01:03:26.490 --> 01:03:28.380
my beliefs about--
- Somalia, Somalia.

01:03:28.380 --> 01:03:30.126
- Oh sorry, that was Somalia, okay.

01:03:30.126 --> 01:03:31.955
- Yeah, Sierra Leone is tomorrow.

01:03:31.955 --> 01:03:33.510
- Okay, all right.

01:03:33.510 --> 01:03:35.360
Okay, I like it even better.
- Yeah.

01:03:36.510 --> 01:03:37.800
- So, that's about norms.

01:03:37.800 --> 01:03:38.633
You know, my beliefs about

01:03:38.633 --> 01:03:40.950
what other people think
I should be doing, right?

01:03:40.950 --> 01:03:42.930
Now, but in the middle part,

01:03:42.930 --> 01:03:45.060
when we were talking
about this aggregate data

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:50.060
and seeing movements from
Pharaonic to Sunna, right?

01:03:50.640 --> 01:03:52.740
I mean, we have these standard problems

01:03:52.740 --> 01:03:54.450
with those aggregate data that's just

01:03:54.450 --> 01:03:56.910
about correlated none
of the rebels, right?

01:03:56.910 --> 01:03:57.743
Like as you said,

01:03:57.743 --> 01:03:59.940
it could just be religious
edict that matter.

01:03:59.940 --> 01:04:02.700
So, all of that could just be signaling

01:04:02.700 --> 01:04:06.390
that what we should be
focusing on is religious edicts

01:04:06.390 --> 01:04:08.940
or changing the law or
something like that, right?

01:04:08.940 --> 01:04:10.800
And that's why in the labor literature,

01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:13.560
you know, how you define

01:04:13.560 --> 01:04:16.470
or how Cristina Bicchieri,
you said, defined norms,

01:04:16.470 --> 01:04:20.220
it sounded like a strategic
complementarity, right?

01:04:20.220 --> 01:04:23.850
My decision to adopt depends
on what other people are doing.

01:04:23.850 --> 01:04:24.930
And there, you know,

01:04:24.930 --> 01:04:26.400
in the peer effects literature, in labor,

01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:28.200
we know that we have to be very careful,

01:04:28.200 --> 01:04:31.470
like the mass and Moretti
type approach on, right?

01:04:31.470 --> 01:04:32.880
So, I just worry,

01:04:32.880 --> 01:04:35.220
I mean, I like the beliefs part,

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:37.380
but I just worry that
if you're not careful

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:38.790
about how we use the word norms,

01:04:38.790 --> 01:04:42.660
maybe we would be
misattributing the importance

01:04:42.660 --> 01:04:46.320
of law changes to focusing
too much on norms.

01:04:46.320 --> 01:04:48.030
- Yeah, so thank you.

01:04:48.030 --> 01:04:50.280
And this helps me clarify something

01:04:50.280 --> 01:04:54.090
which I hope I didn't use causal language,

01:04:54.090 --> 01:04:56.100
when I was describing that middle part,

01:04:56.100 --> 01:04:59.820
if I did, absolutely not intended.

01:04:59.820 --> 01:05:04.820
But so the way I presented
the transition or the flow,

01:05:06.180 --> 01:05:09.090
it's basically indistinguishable for us,

01:05:09.090 --> 01:05:11.520
whether it was governed by the dynamics,

01:05:11.520 --> 01:05:13.623
by the religious leaders action.

01:05:15.790 --> 01:05:17.400
We are not making much out of that.

01:05:17.400 --> 01:05:19.680
We're just saying, "This is a context

01:05:19.680 --> 01:05:21.780
where the three actions make sense."

01:05:21.780 --> 01:05:23.430
If you take historical data,

01:05:23.430 --> 01:05:25.860
you see the switch, that's it.

01:05:25.860 --> 01:05:28.260
What we're trying to use the model for is

01:05:28.260 --> 01:05:30.633
to say with aggregate data,

01:05:31.980 --> 01:05:35.640
what should you see as a precondition

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:39.000
for this now getting you
out of Sunna altogether.

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:43.380
And we are just showing
that we are not seeing that.

01:05:43.380 --> 01:05:45.960
So, it's more like saying on the basis

01:05:45.960 --> 01:05:48.603
of what the model predicts,

01:05:50.490 --> 01:05:53.160
just the evolution of
those stocks and flows,

01:05:53.160 --> 01:05:57.180
is not consistent with the
second step of the transition.

01:05:57.180 --> 01:05:59.640
We're not saying that, you know,

01:05:59.640 --> 01:06:01.770
if religious leaders intervene now,

01:06:01.770 --> 01:06:05.100
Sunna would no longer be absorbing.

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:08.100
So, all of this is for us, I think,

01:06:08.100 --> 01:06:13.100
a way of asking, can you use
this analytical framework

01:06:16.350 --> 01:06:18.540
where there is interdependency

01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:21.840
and where you are just
trying to understand,

01:06:21.840 --> 01:06:25.920
am I in a situation where
there is a form of convexity

01:06:25.920 --> 01:06:30.920
that would generate this pattern
of stocks and flows or not?

01:06:31.290 --> 01:06:33.330
And purely descriptively,

01:06:33.330 --> 01:06:34.980
you're saying this doesn't match it,

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:39.980
but we know that we cannot
attribute a cause and effect,

01:06:41.204 --> 01:06:43.770
especially, to the first part of data,

01:06:43.770 --> 01:06:47.490
because we know there
have been also evolutions

01:06:47.490 --> 01:06:50.940
in the institutional framework
that we are not modeling.

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:54.570
Let me also say why we
decided not to take a path

01:06:54.570 --> 01:06:56.850
where we start modeling incentives

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:58.743
of religious leaders and so on.

01:06:59.970 --> 01:07:01.470
We're trying to see this

01:07:01.470 --> 01:07:04.500
as really as applicable as possible

01:07:04.500 --> 01:07:07.500
to many different types of norms.

01:07:07.500 --> 01:07:10.770
And the moment we get more serious

01:07:10.770 --> 01:07:15.770
and more realistic about the
Somali institutional setting,

01:07:16.410 --> 01:07:18.810
we lose the ability to use the same model

01:07:18.810 --> 01:07:20.640
for e-cigarettes, for example.

01:07:20.640 --> 01:07:22.500
So this comes at a cost,

01:07:22.500 --> 01:07:25.590
we know there are some interesting feature

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:28.440
of what happened there
that we're not capturing,

01:07:28.440 --> 01:07:30.690
but I think, it's still instructive

01:07:30.690 --> 01:07:35.520
to show that even with this
extremely stylized framework,

01:07:35.520 --> 01:07:37.860
we are able to say something

01:07:37.860 --> 01:07:42.860
about what comes next if the
parameters stay constant.

01:07:42.870 --> 01:07:44.250
So, that's another big thing.

01:07:44.250 --> 01:07:47.820
I don't know if that was the
next question by someone,

01:07:47.820 --> 01:07:51.600
but of course, those
conditions and everything says

01:07:51.600 --> 01:07:55.290
that the excess and the
use for the coming years

01:07:55.290 --> 01:07:58.980
to which we extrapolate are
the ones for the past year.

01:07:58.980 --> 01:08:01.083
Big assumption, okay.

01:08:02.940 --> 01:08:04.440
Penny.

01:08:04.440 --> 01:08:07.236
- [Penny] First, I think
this is super interesting.

01:08:07.236 --> 01:08:12.236
I would love to see (indistinct)

01:08:16.350 --> 01:08:18.450
So one type of policy intervention,

01:08:18.450 --> 01:08:20.190
you didn't spend much time

01:08:20.190 --> 01:08:22.890
on is strong paternalistic interventions.

01:08:22.890 --> 01:08:25.530
And here, I'm thinking of smoking.

01:08:25.530 --> 01:08:27.720
So in your model, this utility,

01:08:27.720 --> 01:08:30.810
the cost of all this
practices is extremely high,

01:08:30.810 --> 01:08:34.170
I don't think any woman
or any girl would claim

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:37.800
that they enjoyed the
practice as opposed to smoking

01:08:37.800 --> 01:08:40.439
where most smokers actually love to smoke.

01:08:40.439 --> 01:08:43.080
And so the cost there was low.

01:08:43.080 --> 01:08:44.820
And yet I think somehow we managed,

01:08:44.820 --> 01:08:46.710
at least in the US to
move from an equilibrium

01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:48.330
where everyone smoked to one

01:08:48.330 --> 01:08:51.330
where at least within the educated class,

01:08:51.330 --> 01:08:53.400
most people don't smoke.

01:08:53.400 --> 01:08:54.630
And the only explanation,

01:08:54.630 --> 01:08:56.340
the only policy
intervention I can think of,

01:08:56.340 --> 01:08:58.713
was very strong paternalism.

01:09:00.420 --> 01:09:05.420
So, I'm wondering why you
haven't been more pushy

01:09:06.750 --> 01:09:08.243
on that front.
- Okay.

01:09:11.843 --> 01:09:16.843
- I think, so if you
think of this paternalism,

01:09:17.340 --> 01:09:20.750
it is not obvious that the
appeal of the intermediate norm

01:09:20.750 --> 01:09:24.360
at that point becomes so crucial.

01:09:24.360 --> 01:09:26.520
You might as well try

01:09:26.520 --> 01:09:29.790
and be paternalistic towards
the most desirable state.

01:09:29.790 --> 01:09:34.790
So, we had this idea of thinking
about step-wise processes.

01:09:37.290 --> 01:09:39.900
I also don't know in the smoking case,

01:09:39.900 --> 01:09:42.120
I do agree there was an important role,

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:44.580
played by paternalism.

01:09:44.580 --> 01:09:47.730
You know, I like to think about, you know,

01:09:47.730 --> 01:09:50.310
things like television or, you know,

01:09:50.310 --> 01:09:53.850
and in my view, what happened
with smoking was also

01:09:53.850 --> 01:09:56.820
that it started being
considered less cool.

01:09:56.820 --> 01:09:59.340
So, in terms of how young
people, for example,

01:09:59.340 --> 01:10:01.740
choose whether to smoke or not,

01:10:01.740 --> 01:10:03.330
I think, the image around

01:10:03.330 --> 01:10:08.330
who is a smoker kind of
changed over the years.

01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:12.600
And now, smokers disappeared from movies,

01:10:12.600 --> 01:10:14.160
and now they're back.

01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:19.160
And so I do think, and I'm not
able to quantify in any way,

01:10:20.610 --> 01:10:23.043
you know, this is super,

01:10:25.380 --> 01:10:29.070
not grounded in any serious
research that I've done.

01:10:29.070 --> 01:10:31.590
And maybe there is that I can look at,

01:10:31.590 --> 01:10:33.750
but to me, it was a combination

01:10:33.750 --> 01:10:38.750
of a paternalistic interventions
having certain pictures

01:10:38.850 --> 01:10:42.690
on the packs of cigarettes
and all of that,

01:10:42.690 --> 01:10:45.990
but also, how society started portraying

01:10:45.990 --> 01:10:50.103
and viewing smokers,
no longer so desirable.

01:10:51.384 --> 01:10:56.190
And then I think nowadays,

01:10:56.190 --> 01:11:00.480
strong lobbying to have it back

01:11:00.480 --> 01:11:05.100
in some form of desirable practice.

01:11:05.100 --> 01:11:09.360
But so, yeah, maybe I didn't think I had

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:13.410
so much to say about
paternalistic approaches,

01:11:13.410 --> 01:11:15.960
which doesn't mean that
they're not important,

01:11:15.960 --> 01:11:20.133
similar to the law and
the issue of institutions.

01:11:24.750 --> 01:11:29.750
- So, yeah, to follow up on this question,

01:11:30.690 --> 01:11:32.640
I will maybe frame it a bit differently,

01:11:32.640 --> 01:11:36.270
but talking about not
non-paternalistic approach,

01:11:36.270 --> 01:11:39.510
but top down, like for instance,

01:11:39.510 --> 01:11:42.573
you have a powerful Imam
that changed his mind,

01:11:43.740 --> 01:11:48.577
or you manage to negotiate
with Imam and said,

01:11:48.577 --> 01:11:51.930
"Why don't you pretend
that we are doing this?

01:11:51.930 --> 01:11:55.167
You know, we pay you this,

01:11:55.167 --> 01:11:59.000
and then you tell everyone
else that we are not going

01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:00.540
to do this anymore."

01:12:00.540 --> 01:12:02.910
You know, I think, in West Africa,

01:12:02.910 --> 01:12:05.250
particularly, in Benin, for instance,

01:12:05.250 --> 01:12:07.320
I learned a lot of instances

01:12:07.320 --> 01:12:12.320
where you are not, I mean,
like we want to stop,

01:12:15.210 --> 01:12:20.210
let's say, physical punishment
for some misbehavior.

01:12:21.960 --> 01:12:26.443
And the way to do it is,
you pretend that this is

01:12:27.630 --> 01:12:30.030
what you are punishing
the person physically,

01:12:30.030 --> 01:12:31.380
but then you don't.

01:12:31.380 --> 01:12:33.447
You know, is as if there is,

01:12:34.650 --> 01:12:39.033
it becomes just like a ritual,

01:12:40.470 --> 01:12:44.400
so that you do it, but and
then you are not doing it

01:12:44.400 --> 01:12:46.920
because you minimize, you know?

01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:50.359
So in this context, for instance,

01:12:50.359 --> 01:12:55.359
did you have to deal
with some local leaders

01:12:56.280 --> 01:13:00.100
and try to leverage their influence

01:13:01.050 --> 01:13:05.343
to rethink the whole practice
a little bit, you know?

01:13:05.343 --> 01:13:06.810
Have you run into this?

01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:09.799
- So it's an excellent point.

01:13:09.799 --> 01:13:14.799
In the Somali context, we of
course had to get permission

01:13:16.890 --> 01:13:19.673
to go in the communities,
hold the meetings.

01:13:19.673 --> 01:13:24.673
There was no point of negotiation
or conflict of interest

01:13:24.750 --> 01:13:28.410
because given that we
were not discussing Sunna,

01:13:28.410 --> 01:13:31.394
but we were only talking about Pharaonic,

01:13:31.394 --> 01:13:36.394
there is a full alignment and
there are parts of Somalia

01:13:36.840 --> 01:13:40.890
where Imams have even issued
fat words against Pharaonic.

01:13:40.890 --> 01:13:43.290
In Puntland, for example, in 2018.

01:13:43.290 --> 01:13:45.153
so in that respect,

01:13:46.980 --> 01:13:49.417
we were not in a position where we said,

01:13:49.417 --> 01:13:52.680
"Let's try together to
get out of the practice

01:13:52.680 --> 01:13:56.820
that you are actually
most associated with,

01:13:56.820 --> 01:13:59.130
which is what the Sunna is."

01:13:59.130 --> 01:14:01.620
We did collect the data on circumcisors

01:14:01.620 --> 01:14:05.670
because we also wanted to
understand incentives of the ones

01:14:05.670 --> 01:14:06.630
who practice it.

01:14:06.630 --> 01:14:07.620
And you might think that

01:14:07.620 --> 01:14:12.620
in addition to having the Imams
sanctioning different ways

01:14:14.160 --> 01:14:18.450
of performing the ritual or abandoning it,

01:14:18.450 --> 01:14:22.529
you would also need the
compliance by the circumcisors.

01:14:22.529 --> 01:14:27.450
And so that part to us
is interesting to study.

01:14:27.450 --> 01:14:28.950
I cannot speak for Celine,

01:14:28.950 --> 01:14:33.900
but if I were to say, "What
do you think is gonna work?

01:14:33.900 --> 01:14:37.920
Do you feel that everything
you've tried is, that's it?"

01:14:37.920 --> 01:14:38.940
I wouldn't say so.

01:14:38.940 --> 01:14:42.180
I would think that involving
the local leaders is,

01:14:42.180 --> 01:14:45.420
probably, something that's
first order importance.

01:14:45.420 --> 01:14:50.420
It's something that maybe
requires a lot more embeddedness

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:54.480
in the local context.

01:14:54.480 --> 01:14:58.350
Somalia is a very difficult
context for us to work

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:00.330
in as outsiders.

01:15:00.330 --> 01:15:02.940
And so, but I do agree with you

01:15:02.940 --> 01:15:07.940
that it might be the most
promising strategy on paper.

01:15:08.490 --> 01:15:10.680
In West Africa, it was a bit different

01:15:10.680 --> 01:15:14.640
because we were very careful with Lucia

01:15:14.640 --> 01:15:16.770
to try and avoid as much as possible,

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:21.330
this top-down approach.

01:15:21.330 --> 01:15:23.193
Because in fact, you know,

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:29.490
the local understanding of bondo or FGC,

01:15:29.490 --> 01:15:33.900
is a lot less negative
than what we portrayed,

01:15:33.900 --> 01:15:37.590
and the interaction with
the communities were handled

01:15:37.590 --> 01:15:39.450
by this NGO.

01:15:39.450 --> 01:15:44.430
And this is, it's called
Amazonian Initiative Movement.

01:15:44.430 --> 01:15:46.500
It's actually, for many years,

01:15:46.500 --> 01:15:47.970
they've been acting off the ground.

01:15:47.970 --> 01:15:50.010
And what they do is they try,

01:15:50.010 --> 01:15:53.310
and because it's a secret society thing,

01:15:53.310 --> 01:15:55.650
they try and say, "You choose the sign,

01:15:55.650 --> 01:15:57.090
you choose what you want to do."

01:15:57.090 --> 01:16:00.337
But they try and just
remove the association

01:16:03.810 --> 01:16:06.750
of all the good things of
bondo with the cutting,

01:16:06.750 --> 01:16:08.610
again, what could there be

01:16:08.610 --> 01:16:11.580
in terms of institutional partnerships?

01:16:11.580 --> 01:16:15.600
In that case, we think it's
slightly more complicated.

01:16:15.600 --> 01:16:18.930
The government hasn't
shown a determination

01:16:18.930 --> 01:16:22.740
to go against bondo in
Sierra Leone so far.

01:16:22.740 --> 01:16:24.720
I would love to hear more about Benin

01:16:24.720 --> 01:16:28.380
because that's one context
where we haven't worked.

01:16:28.380 --> 01:16:29.460
Thank you.

01:16:29.460 --> 01:16:31.010
- Okay, I also have a question.

01:16:32.175 --> 01:16:33.750
So, talking a little bit about the law

01:16:33.750 --> 01:16:35.700
and the institutional side things.

01:16:35.700 --> 01:16:40.680
So in Somalia, you didn't
say if it was illegal

01:16:40.680 --> 01:16:41.700
to start with.

01:16:41.700 --> 01:16:46.053
- So I don't think it
is illegal, the Sunna.

01:16:46.973 --> 01:16:47.806
- But the--

01:16:47.806 --> 01:16:50.247
- So, the Pharaonic has
been banned with the--

01:16:51.390 --> 01:16:54.750
- So then, the question is a
little bit on this top down

01:16:54.750 --> 01:16:57.150
or this legal settings.

01:16:57.150 --> 01:17:01.530
It's who are you punishing
or who is the law against?

01:17:01.530 --> 01:17:02.730
Is it against the mother?

01:17:02.730 --> 01:17:06.330
Is it against the doctor who's doing it,

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:09.060
and how can you institutionalize

01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:11.160
and monitor that it happens?

01:17:11.160 --> 01:17:14.820
- It's not easy at all because except,

01:17:14.820 --> 01:17:16.950
I mean, the Sierra Leone is a case

01:17:16.950 --> 01:17:20.280
where monitoring is super
easy because it's a group,

01:17:20.280 --> 01:17:23.190
and you know, if you know
a bunch of girls are gone

01:17:23.190 --> 01:17:24.240
for a month.

01:17:24.240 --> 01:17:29.010
In many other settings, it's done at home.

01:17:29.010 --> 01:17:31.923
It's a very private act.

01:17:33.060 --> 01:17:35.313
The only case I'm familiar with,

01:17:36.420 --> 01:17:38.730
I mentioned it today in our discussion,

01:17:38.730 --> 01:17:40.590
is that of Burkina Faso.

01:17:40.590 --> 01:17:44.940
Burkina also has a practice of FGC,

01:17:44.940 --> 01:17:47.133
but it recent years,

01:17:48.480 --> 01:17:51.390
its banned and the government has decided

01:17:51.390 --> 01:17:53.520
to enforce the law.

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:58.520
So Lucia and I initially thought
about doing an intervention

01:18:01.680 --> 01:18:06.680
in Burkina, and we started some
focus group and pilot work,

01:18:08.310 --> 01:18:09.900
and it became clear to us

01:18:09.900 --> 01:18:13.770
that people didn't want to talk about it.

01:18:13.770 --> 01:18:17.250
They were extremely
reluctant to discuss FGC.

01:18:17.250 --> 01:18:19.620
They said, "There's no
FGC in our communities."

01:18:19.620 --> 01:18:23.933
And we later learned that
there had been trials

01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:29.220
that had been brought to the rural area.

01:18:29.220 --> 01:18:32.610
So instead of, so both the family

01:18:32.610 --> 01:18:34.800
and the circumcisors were punished,

01:18:34.800 --> 01:18:37.740
and instead of holding
them in the capital,

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:42.390
they were on purpose held in locations

01:18:42.390 --> 01:18:44.310
where the population would become aware.

01:18:44.310 --> 01:18:45.910
And this apparently,

01:18:45.910 --> 01:18:49.410
at least in terms of
willingness to discuss it

01:18:49.410 --> 01:18:51.570
or to report it, had an effect.

01:18:51.570 --> 01:18:54.450
I don't know if it had an
effect on cutting rates,

01:18:54.450 --> 01:18:58.140
but, and we haven't gone back to study

01:18:58.140 --> 01:19:02.394
why they became serious about
enforcing how they did it.

01:19:02.394 --> 01:19:05.223
We read about these trials,

01:19:05.223 --> 01:19:07.323
but I do think that is, you know,

01:19:08.760 --> 01:19:12.240
it's one instance where finding out

01:19:12.240 --> 01:19:15.990
about it would probably
come from some monitoring

01:19:15.990 --> 01:19:20.990
of the circumcisors and then
probably that's how they do it.

01:19:27.720 --> 01:19:30.840
- Thanks for the really
interesting presentation.

01:19:30.840 --> 01:19:33.240
In your model, one thing I
was interested in was the fact

01:19:33.240 --> 01:19:36.810
that the reference group is
kind of exogenously given,

01:19:36.810 --> 01:19:38.430
and I know this is outside the scope

01:19:38.430 --> 01:19:40.085
of what you talked about,

01:19:40.085 --> 01:19:41.400
but maybe if you had
some speculative thoughts

01:19:41.400 --> 01:19:44.490
about what happens when
reference groups come

01:19:44.490 --> 01:19:46.050
into contact with each other,

01:19:46.050 --> 01:19:49.440
or are there cases where
some subgroup might decide

01:19:49.440 --> 01:19:52.440
to split off and kind of divorce itself

01:19:52.440 --> 01:19:55.533
from the broader reference
group to protect a norm?

01:19:56.793 --> 01:19:58.800
- Yeah, that's a great question.

01:19:58.800 --> 01:19:59.943
In fact, you know,

01:20:00.810 --> 01:20:05.810
almost by definition, if you
wanted to split the population

01:20:06.330 --> 01:20:08.790
into, now my reference
group are the people

01:20:08.790 --> 01:20:11.430
who are choosing my action, I pay no cost.

01:20:11.430 --> 01:20:14.958
You know, I'm always free to deviate.

01:20:14.958 --> 01:20:19.958
So, I think the problem is
interesting to the extent

01:20:20.550 --> 01:20:23.400
that you cannot fully
redefine the reference group

01:20:23.400 --> 01:20:27.360
in a way to align it to
your own preferences.

01:20:27.360 --> 01:20:32.040
Now, is there a feasible way
from an analytical perspective

01:20:32.040 --> 01:20:36.660
to study an intermediate version

01:20:36.660 --> 01:20:40.620
that's not the full population
health constant as I did,

01:20:40.620 --> 01:20:44.010
not the extreme in which my
reference group is always

01:20:44.010 --> 01:20:45.870
whoever thinks like me,

01:20:45.870 --> 01:20:50.645
but there's a partition that's different.

01:20:50.645 --> 01:20:55.200
I'll be honest, I haven't
seen anything like this,

01:20:55.200 --> 01:20:59.280
at least in the social
norms literature, of course,

01:20:59.280 --> 01:21:02.010
people working on
coalitions might have tools

01:21:02.010 --> 01:21:03.213
that come in handy.

01:21:04.350 --> 01:21:06.900
And I don't know whether any

01:21:06.900 --> 01:21:10.650
of what we've done would
generalize to that setting.

01:21:10.650 --> 01:21:14.220
In principle, it should make
it easier to transition on

01:21:14.220 --> 01:21:17.400
because you're always
decreasing the punishment

01:21:17.400 --> 01:21:20.820
if you strategically redefine
your reference group.

01:21:20.820 --> 01:21:23.850
But you know, there are,

01:21:23.850 --> 01:21:26.010
if these references groups are there

01:21:26.010 --> 01:21:31.010
because they also provide,
so they have a function,

01:21:31.320 --> 01:21:33.690
you want to have a large enough group

01:21:33.690 --> 01:21:36.750
that you can do risk sharing with or so.

01:21:36.750 --> 01:21:39.660
So there are economies of scale

01:21:39.660 --> 01:21:43.170
to some extent in keeping these groups not

01:21:43.170 --> 01:21:46.800
as small as it renders costless,

01:21:46.800 --> 01:21:48.810
these particular action choice.

01:21:48.810 --> 01:21:52.736
But analytically, I
haven't seen anything going

01:21:52.736 --> 01:21:54.360
in that direction.

01:21:54.360 --> 01:21:57.363
I agree, it seems a very,
very interesting question.

01:21:58.350 --> 01:22:00.850
- [Rohini] I think, we can
take one more question.

01:22:10.860 --> 01:22:11.693
- [Man] Thank you very much

01:22:11.693 --> 01:22:13.590
for the fascinating presentation.

01:22:13.590 --> 01:22:17.430
I had a question on sort of
the risks of a norm reversal,

01:22:17.430 --> 01:22:18.630
particularly, within communities

01:22:18.630 --> 01:22:20.670
where there's sort of an
embracing of traditional ideas.

01:22:20.670 --> 01:22:23.160
Do we see cases where
like over generations

01:22:23.160 --> 01:22:26.150
as re-adoption of harmful
norms, for example,

01:22:26.150 --> 01:22:28.260
in cases where grandmothers
or mothers are uncut

01:22:28.260 --> 01:22:30.540
or undergo sort of Sunna,

01:22:30.540 --> 01:22:33.053
but then their children
undergo Pharaonic cases?

01:22:34.200 --> 01:22:37.170
- So we haven't seen that
reversal in the data.

01:22:37.170 --> 01:22:42.170
In fact, the trend is always for

01:22:43.890 --> 01:22:46.710
in our data, mothers
maybe having Pharaonic

01:22:46.710 --> 01:22:48.207
and daughter Sunna,

01:22:48.207 --> 01:22:51.600
and we never see the opposite in the data.

01:22:51.600 --> 01:22:53.250
I do think that in theory,

01:22:53.250 --> 01:22:57.330
you could actually have that case

01:22:57.330 --> 01:22:59.250
where there's some backlash,

01:22:59.250 --> 01:23:01.980
again, maybe coming out of our framework.

01:23:01.980 --> 01:23:05.880
In our framework, because of
that ranking of utilities,

01:23:05.880 --> 01:23:08.100
by definition, if you manage

01:23:08.100 --> 01:23:11.490
to overcome the social sanctions
and you got to apply point

01:23:11.490 --> 01:23:13.860
where the intrinsic utility is higher,

01:23:13.860 --> 01:23:15.900
there's no reason to move from there.

01:23:15.900 --> 01:23:18.300
So, you would need some extraneous element

01:23:18.300 --> 01:23:20.160
to the theory I presented,

01:23:20.160 --> 01:23:24.390
which empirically, I think, could happen

01:23:24.390 --> 01:23:27.900
in cases with the backlash.

01:23:27.900 --> 01:23:30.200
But yeah, thank you.

01:23:34.219 --> 01:23:36.330
- Thank you, Eliana, for a great talk.

01:23:36.330 --> 01:23:39.210
And I'd invite everyone to
join us now for reception,

01:23:39.210 --> 01:23:42.330
which I think is in the
new economics building.

01:23:42.330 --> 01:23:44.767
So, thank you very much.
- Thank you, Rohini.

01:23:44.767 --> 01:23:47.934
(audience applauding)

01:23:55.562 --> 01:23:58.145
(gentle music)

